//! member: false
//!
//! timeout — run a command, and signal it if it is still running after a
//! time.
//!
//! References consulted: GNU coreutils 9.11 `timeout --help`, the info
//! manual's `timeout invocation` node, and black-box runs of the GNU
//! binary in throwaway containers (linux x86-64, `notes/bu27/probes/
//! tprobe.sh`, kasumi `~/lanes/bu27/ev/tprobe-gnu.log`) and on CI's macOS
//! runner, under `LC_ALL=C`; POSIX.1-2024 for `kill(2)`, `setpgid(2)` and
//! `waitpid(2)`. Never GNU source, nor any other `timeout`'s.
//!
//! GNU's rules, each measured against 9.11:
//!
//! - **The command line.** `timeout [OPTION]... DURATION COMMAND [ARG]...`.
//!   Options end at the first operand, whatever `POSIXLY_CORRECT` says:
//!   `timeout 1 -s KILL true` runs the command `-s`. `-f`/`--foreground`,
//!   `-k`/`--kill-after=DURATION`, `-p`/`--preserve-status`,
//!   `-s`/`--signal=SIGNAL`, `-v`/`--verbose`, `--help`, `--version`. A
//!   DURATION is `sleep`'s (`bore/duration.lu`); 0 disables the timeout,
//!   and a non-zero one too small for a double is the shortest there is.
//!   A word like `-1` is an option, so a negative DURATION cannot be
//!   written. A SIGNAL is `kill`'s (`bore/signals.lu`), 0 included.
//! - **Status 125** for timeout's own failures, each said where it is
//!   met: an invalid DURATION (`invalid time interval 'X'`), an invalid
//!   SIGNAL (`'X': invalid signal`), a bad option (getopt's words). Too
//!   few operands print only the `Try` line. **126** for a command found
//!   but not runnable and **127** for one not found (`failed to run
//!   command 'X': REASON`). A file without `#!` is run by `/bin/sh`.
//! - **The group.** Unless `--foreground`, timeout first leads a new
//!   process group (`setpgid(0, 0)`; a session leader cannot move and
//!   stays its own group's leader), and the command starts in it: at the
//!   expiry the signal goes to the command and then to the whole group,
//!   so the command's own children end too. timeout itself does not end
//!   by that send. With `--foreground` timeout stays in its parent's
//!   group and only the command is signalled.
//! - **At the expiry** the signal (TERM unless `-s`) is sent, then CONT
//!   (unless the signal was KILL, CONT or STOP), so a stopped command
//!   ends. A DURATION under a millisecond has expired by the time the
//!   command is started.
//!   `-k D` sends KILL D after that, to the group as well. `-v` says each
//!   send on standard error: `timeout: sending signal TERM to command
//!   'sleep'` (signal 0, and one with no name, by number).
//! - **Signals sent to timeout.** HUP, INT, QUIT, TERM, USR1, USR2, PIPE,
//!   ABRT, XCPU, XFSZ, VTALRM and PROF are passed to the command (and its
//!   group) as they come, and arm `-k` as the expiry does; ALRM is the
//!   expiry itself (124). TTIN and TTOU are ignored, in timeout and not
//!   in the command. CHLD, CONT, WINCH and URG change nothing; TSTP stops
//!   timeout.
//! - **The status.** The command's own, when it ends by itself; when a
//!   signal ended it, timeout ends by that signal too (its parent reads a
//!   death by TERM, not an exit status of 143). After the expiry: 124,
//!   or the command's status with `-p` (a death by signal N as the exit
//!   status 128+N); a command ended by the KILL timeout sent is 137
//!   either way, and without `--foreground` that KILL ends timeout too,
//!   so its parent reads a death by KILL.
//! - Descriptors pass to the command as they are: standard input
//!   (closed, if timeout's was), output, error, and any other descriptor
//!   timeout was started with.
//!
//! **What this `timeout` cannot do, each refused by name:**
//!
//! - **Send a signal it cannot ignore to its own group.** GNU's timeout
//!   ignores the signal while it sends it to the group it leads; wolf can
//!   ignore only the twenty listenable meanings (`[os.signal.disp]`), not
//!   the six fault signals (`-s SEGV`, `ILL`, `TRAP`, `BUS`, `FPE`, `SYS`)
//!   nor a host signal (`-s RTMIN`, `STKFLT`, …), and the send would end
//!   timeout itself. Without `--foreground` such a SIGNAL is status 125,
//!   by name; with it, it is sent to the command alone, as GNU sends it.
//! - **Pass on a signal it cannot hear.** GNU's timeout also catches the
//!   fault signals and the host's real-time and host-only signals sent to
//!   it, and passes them on; wolf cannot listen for them, so each ends
//!   timeout by its default action and the command runs on.
//! - **Say a core was dumped.** GNU adds `the monitored command dumped
//!   core` when the command's death left one; `os_wait_poll` answers the
//!   signal and not the core.
//! - **Group 1.** wolf never signals process group 1
//!   (`[os.signal.send.group]`): a timeout that cannot leave group 1 (a
//!   container's first process, a session leader) signals only its
//!   command.
//! - A timeout at its descriptor limit does not start (the signal hub's
//!   rule, `[os.signal.hub]`: exit 71).

use bore
use std.signal

fn main() -> int {
    let p = bore.parse(options(), env_args(), false)
    var out = bore.stdout()
    let table = bore.signal_table()
    var entry = bore.signal_entry(table, "TERM") else -1
    var kill_after = bore.Duration { secs: 0.0, tiny: false }
    var foreground = false
    var preserve = false
    var verbose = false
    var i = 0
    while i < p.keys.len {
        let k = p.keys[i]
        let v = p.values[i]
        i += 1
        if k == "help" {
            bore.put(mut out, help()) else |_| {}
            return bore.finish(mut out, "timeout", 0)
        }
        if k == "version" {
            bore.put(mut out, bore.version_text("timeout")) else |_| {}
            return bore.finish(mut out, "timeout", 0)
        }
        if k == "signal" {
            entry = bore.signal_entry(table, v) else |_| {
                bore.warn("timeout", "{bore.quote(v)}: invalid signal")
                return try_help()
            }
        } else if k == "kill-after" {
            kill_after = bore.duration_of(v) else |_| {
                bore.warn("timeout", "invalid time interval {bore.quote(v)}")
                return try_help()
            }
        } else if k == "foreground" {
            foreground = true
        } else if k == "preserve-status" {
            preserve = true
        } else if k == "verbose" {
            verbose = true
        }
    }
    if !bore.ok(p) {
        bore.warn("timeout", p.error)
        return try_help()
    }
    if p.operands.len < 2 {
        return try_help()
    }
    let limit = bore.duration_of(p.operands[0]) else |_| {
        bore.warn("timeout", "invalid time interval {bore.quote(p.operands[0])}")
        return try_help()
    }
    let sig = bore.signal_meaning(table, entry)
    let unheard = sig != 0 && !bore.signal_listenable(table, entry)
    if !foreground && unheard && sig != signal.kill() && sig != signal.stop() {
        bore.warn(
            "timeout",
            "{bore.quote(bore.signal_name(table, entry))}: wolf cannot ignore this signal, so timeout cannot send it to the group it leads ([os.signal.disp]); with --foreground it is sent to the command alone",
        )
        return 125
    }
    let cmd = p.operands[1]
    var rest = List[str]()
    var a = 2
    while a < p.operands.len {
        (mut rest).push(p.operands[a])
        a += 1
    }
    run(
        Run {
            table: table,
            entry: entry,
            cmd: cmd,
            limit: limit,
            kill_after: kill_after,
            foreground: foreground,
            preserve: preserve,
            verbose: verbose,
        },
        rest,
    )
}

/// What the command line asks for, once read.
struct Run {
    table: bore.SignalTable,
    entry: int,
    cmd: str,
    limit: bore.Duration,
    kill_after: bore.Duration,
    foreground: bool,
    preserve: bool,
    verbose: bool,
}

/// The meanings timeout hears and passes on to its command, as GNU's
/// passes on what it catches (the header): HUP, INT, QUIT, TERM, USR1,
/// USR2, PIPE, ABRT, XCPU, XFSZ, VTALRM, PROF.
fn passed_on() -> int {
    signal.reload() |
        signal.interrupt() |
        signal.quit() |
        signal.terminate() |
        signal.user() |
        signal.upgrade() |
        signal.broken_pipe() |
        signal.abort() |
        signal.cpu_limit() |
        signal.file_limit() |
        signal.virtual_alarm() |
        signal.profile_alarm()
}

/// Start the command and see it to its end; the status timeout ends
/// with (when it does not end by a signal itself).
fn run(r: Run, args: List[str]) -> int {
    if !r.foreground {
        // A session leader cannot move (`denied`); it already leads its
        // own group, which is what this is for.
        os_set_pgid(0) else |_| {}
    }
    os_signal_ignore(signal.bg_read() | signal.bg_write()) else |_| {}
    let heard = passed_on() | signal.child() | signal.alarm()
    os_signal_listen(heard) else |_| {
        bore.warn("timeout", "cannot listen for signals")
        return 125
    }
    // Standard input passes as it is, closed if timeout's was.
    var map = List[int]()
    (mut map).push(0)
    let open0 = fs_fstat(0) else |_| {
        List[int]()
    }
    (mut map).push(if open0.len > 0 then 0 else -1)
    let h = os_spawn_job(r.cmd, args, map, -1, -1, signal.bg_read() | signal.bg_write()) else |e| {
        var why = "cannot be run, and wolf does not say why (wolf-lang#682)"
        var code = 126
        match e {
            not_found => {
                why = "No such file or directory"
                code = 127
            },
            denied => {
                why = "Permission denied"
            },
            invalid => {},
            io => {},
            unsupported => {
                why = "this host cannot start a command"
            },
        }
        bore.warn("timeout", "failed to run command {bore.quote(r.cmd)}: {why}")
        return code
    }
    let child = os_proc_pid(h) else -1
    var timed_out = false
    var sent = false
    var killing = false
    scope s {
        if r.limit.secs > 0.0 || r.limit.tiny {
            let ms = millis(r.limit)
            if ms == 0 {
                // Shorter than wolf's millisecond: expired already, as
                // GNU's nanosecond timer is before its command has run.
                os_signal_raise(signal.alarm()) else |_| {}
            } else {
                s.spawn(fn() {
                    alarm_after(ms)
                })
            }
        }
        loop {
            let (k, st) = os_wait_poll() else |_| {
                (-2, 0)
            }
            if k == h && st < 256 {
                os_exit(ended(r, st, timed_out))
            }
            if k == -2 {
                // The table answered `io`: the host reaped the command
                // itself, so how it ended is gone.
                os_exit(if timed_out then 124 else 125)
            }
            if k >= 0 {
                // A stop (256 + N) is no ending: wait on.
                continue
            }
            let m = os_signal_wait(heard) else |_| {
                0
            }
            if m == 0 || m == signal.child() {
                continue
            }
            if m == signal.alarm() && sent {
                // The second alarm: `-k`'s.
                if killing {
                    let e = bore.signal_entry(r.table, "KILL") else -1
                    send(r, e, child)
                    killing = false
                }
                continue
            }
            if m == signal.alarm() {
                timed_out = true
                send(r, r.entry, child)
            } else {
                send(r, entry_of(r.table, m), child)
            }
            sent = true
            if (r.kill_after.secs > 0.0 || r.kill_after.tiny) && !killing {
                killing = true
                let kms = millis(r.kill_after)
                s.spawn(fn() {
                    alarm_after(kms)
                })
            }
        }
    }
    125
}

/// A DURATION in whole milliseconds, rounded up; 0 (at once) for one
/// shorter than a millisecond, which GNU's nanosecond timer ends about
/// as soon (wolf sleeps in milliseconds); a duration past what an `int`
/// of milliseconds holds is the largest wait there is.
fn millis(d: bore.Duration) -> int {
    let ms = d.secs * 1000.0
    if ms < 1.0 {
        return 0
    }
    if ms > 9000000000000000000.0 {
        return 9000000000000000000
    }
    var whole = ms as int
    if whole as f64 < ms {
        whole += 1
    }
    whole
}

/// Sleep `ms` milliseconds, then deliver ALARM to this process: the
/// expiry is a delivered meaning the wait sees, as GNU's is a SIGALRM.
fn alarm_after(ms: int) {
    var left = ms
    while left > 1000000000 {
        time_sleep_ms(1000000000)
        left -= 1000000000
    }
    if left > 0 {
        time_sleep_ms(left)
    }
    os_signal_raise(signal.alarm()) else |_| {}
}

/// The table's entry for a meaning timeout heard.
fn entry_of(t: bore.SignalTable, m: int) -> int {
    var i = 0
    while i < t.numbers.len {
        if bore.signal_meaning(t, i) == m {
            return i
        }
        i += 1
    }
    -1
}

/// Send entry `e`'s signal to the command `child`, then (unless
/// `--foreground`) to the group timeout leads, ignoring it here for the
/// length of that send; then CONT the same way, so a stopped command
/// hears it, unless the signal was KILL or CONT.
fn send(r: Run, e: int, child: int) {
    let m = bore.signal_meaning(r.table, e)
    if r.verbose {
        bore.warn(
            "timeout",
            "sending signal {verbose_name(r.table, e)} to command {bore.quote(r.cmd)}",
        )
    }
    to_all(r, r.table, e, m, child)
    if m != signal.kill() && m != signal.continued() && m != signal.stop() {
        let c = bore.signal_entry(r.table, "CONT") else -1
        to_all(r, r.table, c, signal.continued(), child)
    }
}

/// The name `-v` gives a signal (measured): its name, or its number for
/// signal 0 and for one with no name.
fn verbose_name(t: bore.SignalTable, e: int) -> str {
    if e >= 0 && bore.signal_named(t, e) {
        return bore.signal_name(t, e)
    }
    "{bore.signal_number(t, e)}"
}

fn to_all(r: Run, t: bore.SignalTable, e: int, m: int, child: int) {
    if child > 0 {
        os_signal_send(child, m) else |_| {}
    }
    if r.foreground {
        return
    }
    let own = os_pgid() else |_| {
        return
    }
    if own <= 1 {
        return
    }
    let quiet = bore.signal_listenable(t, e)
    if quiet {
        os_signal_ignore(m) else |_| {}
    }
    os_signal_send_group(own, m) else |_| {}
    if quiet {
        if (passed_on() | signal.child() | signal.alarm()) & m != 0 {
            os_signal_listen(m) else |_| {}
        } else if m != signal.bg_read() && m != signal.bg_write() {
            os_signal_default(m) else |_| {}
        }
    }
}

/// How timeout ends once its command has, `st` the poll's status: the
/// command's own, or 124 after the expiry (the header).
fn ended(r: Run, st: int, timed_out: bool) -> int {
    if st >= 0 {
        if timed_out && !r.preserve {
            return 124
        }
        return st
    }
    let n = -st
    let kill = os_signal_number(signal.kill()) else 9
    if timed_out {
        if n == kill {
            return 128 + n
        }
        if r.preserve {
            return 128 + n
        }
        return 124
    }
    // Ended by a signal of its own: end by the same one.
    let e = bore.signal_entry(r.table, "{n}") else -1
    let m = bore.signal_meaning(r.table, e)
    if e >= 0 && m != 0 {
        if bore.signal_listenable(r.table, e) {
            os_signal_default(m) else |_| {}
        }
        let me = os_pid()
        os_signal_send(me, m) else |_| {}
        time_sleep_ms(100)
    }
    128 + n
}

/// GNU's `Try` line, and its status.
fn try_help() -> int {
    eprint("Try 'timeout --help' for more information.")
    125
}

fn options() -> List[bore.Opt] {
    var s = List[bore.Opt]()
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("f", "foreground"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("k", "kill-after"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("p", "preserve-status"))
    (mut s).push(bore.takes("s", "signal"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("v", "verbose"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "help"))
    (mut s).push(bore.flag("", "version"))
    s
}

fn help() -> str {
    """
    Usage: timeout [OPTION]... DURATION COMMAND [ARG]...
    Run COMMAND, and signal it if it is still running after DURATION.

      -f, --foreground
            leave timeout in its parent's process group, so COMMAND may
            read the terminal and hear its signals; only COMMAND itself is
            signalled, not the processes it started
      -k, --kill-after=DURATION
            send KILL too, if COMMAND still runs this long after the first
            signal
      -p, --preserve-status
            end with COMMAND's own status, even after a timeout
      -s, --signal=SIGNAL
            the signal to send at the timeout (TERM when none is named),
            by name or by number, as kill -l lists them
      -v, --verbose
            say on standard error each signal sent at a timeout
          --help     print this help and exit
          --version  print the version and exit

    DURATION is a number, as for sleep, with an optional s (seconds, the
    default), m (minutes), h (hours) or d (days). 0 means no timeout.

    The status is 124 after a timeout (COMMAND's own with -p), 125 when
    timeout itself fails, 126 when COMMAND cannot be run, 127 when it
    cannot be found, 137 when KILL ended it, and COMMAND's own otherwise.
    Without --foreground this timeout refuses a SIGNAL it cannot ignore
    (the fault signals, the host's real-time ones).

    boreutils: https://github.com/wolffe-lang/boreutils
    """
}
